     "style": "warn",
     "title": "The wire no longer rests low",
     "text": "A released pin has only its internal pull-down, 50 to 80 kΩ. Against 2.2 kΩ it decides nothing."
    },
    {
     "from": 31.060000000000002,
     "to": 41.21,
     "anchor": [
      "c",
      "rpu"
     ],
     "at": [
      1110,
      170
     ],
     "style": "warn",
     "title": "5 V with nothing plugged in",
     "text": "The wire follows the 5 V supply through the resistor, whatever protocol the pin is to speak next."
    },
    {
     "from": 41.809999999999995,
     "to": 49.300000000000004,
     "anchor": [
      "c",
      "line"
     ],
     "at": [
      351,
      700
     ],
     "side": "t",
     "title": "One pin, several protocols",
     "text": "A pulse protocol and a digital stream rest on a low wire. The pull-up serves one protocol and changes the wire for all."
    }
   ],
   "say": [
    {
     "at": 0.8,
     "text": "The textbook answer is a pull-up resistor: 2.2 kilohms from the wire to a 5 volt supply."
    },
    {
     "at": 8.22,
     "text": "Pull-up and pull-down form a divider. The released wire rises to 2.9 volts, and the answer appears."
    },
    {
     "at": 16.45,
     "text": "But a resistor is there all the time, for every protocol on the pin. Whenever the pin lets go, at reset or between two protocols, the wire no longer rests low. It sits at 2.9 volts."
    },
    {
     "at": 30.76,
     "text": "With nothing plugged in, it is the full 5 volts. The other protocols expect a wire that rests low, and a level the pin alone decides."
    },
    {
     "at": 41.51,
     "text": "One protocol needs the resistor. The others cannot have it. So the resistor cannot be fitted."
    }
   ]
  },
